import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import RagDiagram from '../../components/RagDiagram.astro';
import Sidenote from '../../components/Sidenote.astro';
---

<Base
  title="YouTube competitive intelligence — Shangwei Yang"
  description="An end-to-end RAG system on GCP that turns weekly YouTube data from ten news channels into grounded editorial insight for the TaiwanPlus newsroom."
>
  <article class="prose has-margin">
    <p class="eyebrow">Case study · 2026 · TaiwanPlus</p>
    <h1>YouTube competitive intelligence, retrieval-augmented</h1>
    <p class="meta">
      Python · BigQuery · Vertex AI Search · Gemini 3.5 Flash · Streamlit · Cloud Run ·
      <a href="https://github.com/shangweiyang79/AI_project">github repo</a>
    </p>

    <p class="lede">
      Every week, editors at TaiwanPlus wanted to know what was working for about ten competing
      international news channels — and answering that meant someone manually scanning channel
      pages for a day. I replaced the scan with a question box, and editors' decision time fell
      30%. The hard part wasn't the chat; it was knowing whether an answer was fit to ship.
    </p>

    <figure>
      <RagDiagram />
      <figcaption>
        <span class="fig-no">Fig. 1</span>One pipeline, one command: weekly Comscore/YouTube
        exports (~20K videos, deduplicated by video ID; ~10K after cleaning) are enriched with
        velocity metrics, loaded into BigQuery, indexed by Vertex AI Search, and served through
        Gemini to a Streamlit chat on Cloud Run. Every answer is checked before it ships.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Velocity: finding breakouts before the totals do</h2>
    <p>
      Raw view counts reward old videos. The pipeline computes
      <code>velocity_daily</code> (views per day since publish) and
      <code>recent_velocity</code> (the short-window trend)<Sidenote id="vel">
        A three-year-old explainer with 2M views is history; a three-day-old video pacing 80k/day
        is intelligence. The two metrics separate exactly those cases.
      </Sidenote> so editors see what is spiking <em>now</em>, not what accumulated. Breakout
      detection is a sort, not a model — the leverage was defining the right metric.
    </p>

    <h2>Evaluating an answer with no single right answer</h2>
    <p>
      The output is a strategic brief — three trending topics, why each went viral, what to cover
      next — so there is nothing to diff against. But "no correct answer" is not "no wrong
      answers", and the wrong ones turned out to be mechanically detectable. Each brief splits
      into claims that can be <em>verified</em> (this video exists; it has this many views) and
      claims that can only be <em>judged</em> (it went viral because of emotional resonance). The
      rule: never ask an LLM to verify a number it cannot recompute.<Sidenote id="ground">
        Two query modes serve different trust needs: the Answer API synthesizes a strategic brief
        with citations; the Search API returns ranked, filterable raw results when editors want to
        see the evidence themselves.
      </Sidenote>
    </p>
    <p>
      Eight deterministic checks verify every citation and number against the source database
      after each answer — catching the same documentary cited three times as three videos, an
      "Accelerating" label on a video with no trend data, and "trends" resting on a single
      source. A golden set computed from each weekly snapshot defines which videos should qualify,
      and a gate sends back any answer with three or more findings instead of shipping it.
    </p>
    <p>
      On the labelled set, the gate catches 90% of the answers a reviewer rejected while passing
      80% of the ones they accepted.<Sidenote id="caveat">
        Stated plainly: that threshold was fitted on the same 20 answers, labelled by one reviewer.
        It is a defensible starting point, not a validated constant. A rubric-based LLM judge is
        implemented but parked — on the first labels it did not separate accepted from rejected
        answers, while the deterministic counts did.
      </Sidenote> In an editorial setting this isn't a nice-to-have — an ungrounded "insight" that
      reaches a pitch meeting is worse than no tool at all.
    </p>

    <h2>Closing the loop: from query to slide deck</h2>
    <p>
      Insight that stays in a chat window dies there. A <code>python-pptx</code> module renders the
      recurring analyses — content gaps, velocity leaders, category trends — into the branded
      biweekly deck the newsroom already uses, so the system's output lands directly in the
      existing editorial routine.
    </p>

    <h2>Stack</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Layer</th><th>Technology</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Ingestion</td><td>Python, pandas — one-command pipeline (<code>run_pipeline.py</code>)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Warehouse</td><td>Google BigQuery</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Retrieval</td><td>Vertex AI Search (synced from BigQuery via a Cloud Run service)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Generation</td><td>Gemini 3.5 Flash, grounded on retrieved sources</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Evaluation</td><td>8 deterministic checks, computed golden set, ship/send-back gate; 23 tests over the checks</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Interface</td><td>Streamlit chat with preset newsroom queries</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Deployment</td><td>Cloud Run, serverless</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Reporting</td><td>python-pptx auto-generated decks</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>What this project demonstrates</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Full ownership of an ML product</strong> — ingestion to deployment to the deck on the editor's desk, not a notebook that stops at a metric.</li>
      <li><strong>Evals for open-ended output</strong> — verify what can be verified, gate on it, and keep the LLM judge parked until humans calibrate it.</li>
      <li><strong>Metric design as analysis</strong> — velocity turned a data dump into a detection system.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="meta">
      Code and pipeline scripts:
      <a href="https://github.com/shangweiyang79/AI_project">shangweiyang79/AI_project</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

